abstract: "We give a self-contained elementary proof of the Liouville--Goldbach theorem (LG): every even N > 2 is a sum of two positive integers at which the Liouville function equals -1. The proof is independent of Mangerel's correlation bound and of GRH. We repair a p = 7 boundary in the written commuting-square argument, audit the two September 2026 manuscripts, and machine-check the logical boundary: binary Goldbach is equivalent to the strengthening LG_{Omega <= 2}. The upstream Lean 4 development is rebuilt at source level under Lean 4.33.0-rc1 (2851 jobs); the two audit modules return 0 and the main theorem depends only on propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound. A third module, IntervalLemma.lean, kernel-checks that for every prime p >= 11 the interval (p/6, p/4) contains an integer (namely n = p/4), closing the non-emptiness side of the written commuting-square step. A finite check covers all even N <= 5,000,000 with zero failures. We do NOT prove binary Goldbach; it remains open. We make no priority claim; the LG route and the Lean development come from the September 2026 upstream manuscripts. The audit of the 'multiples of four' manuscript is version-sensitive: the manuscript cites the published IMRN version, whose abstract states the bound for all N >= 11, while arXiv v2 states it only for sufficiently large N with an ineffective N0. We checked only the published abstract, not the published body line by line; the two thresholds must not substitute for each other. A 25 September 2026 MathOverflow answer by L. Meng states a stronger unconditional result (all four sign pairs for every N not in {2,3,4,5,6,9,10}); this paper does not address that result and makes no priority claim over it. We withdraw an earlier claim that the manuscript committed a quantifier substitution."
